{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecificLimits.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Normed.Field.Lemmas"],"declarations":56,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.cauchyMul_order_noCancellation_insufficient","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.cauchyMul_order_noCancellation_insufficient","kind":"theorem","line":1641,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L1641-L1688","doc":"**`NoCancellation` alone does NOT rescue the convolution law** (machine-proved,\nno `sorry`, no external witness).  Adding `hnc : NoCancellation a b` to the\nhypothesis-free `cauchyMul_order_unhypothesised_false` does **not** make the law\ntrue — the same `b ≡ 0` witness satisfies `NoCancellation` *vacuously*.  Over `ℝ`\nwith `q = 2`: `a n = 2^{n²}` (order `2`), `b ≡ 0` (order `0`); then `a ⋆ b = 0`, so\n`c·‖a_k‖·‖b_{n-k}‖ = c·‖a_k‖·0 = 0 ≤ 0 = ‖(a⋆b)_n‖` — `NoCancellation` holds — yet\n`a ⋆ b = 0` has order `0` while `cauchyEnvelope 2 0 = max 2 0 = 2`.  `order_unique`\ngives `0 = 2`.\n\nThis is why the fixed `cauchyMul_order` carries the **everywhere-nonzero**\nhypotheses `∀ n, a n ≠ 0` / `∀ n, b n ≠ 0` in addition to `hnc`: non-cancellation\nbounds the *product's* norm below by its summands, but is vacuous when a *factor*\nvanishes, so it cannot by itself force the `≥` half.  Axioms\n`[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.cauchyMul_order_unhypothesised_false","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.cauchyMul_order_unhypothesised_false","kind":"theorem","line":1605,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L1605-L1639","doc":"**The unhypothesised convolution law is FALSE** (machine-proved, no `sorry`,\nno external witness).  Over `ℝ` with `q = 2`: `a n = 2^{n²}` has order `2`, the\nzero sequence has order `0`, their Cauchy product is `0` and so has order `0`,\nyet `cauchyEnvelope 2 0 = max 2 0 = 2`.  `order_unique` then gives `0 = 2`.\n\nThis is why `cauchyMul_order` carries `NoCancellation`.  Axioms\n`[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.isQGevreyOrder_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.isQGevreyOrder_zero","kind":"theorem","line":1597,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L1597-L1603","doc":"**The identically-zero sequence has q-Gevrey order `0`**, for every base:\n`‖0‖ = 0` and `Real.log 0 = 0`, so the defining ratio is identically `0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.cauchyMul_order","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.cauchyMul_order","kind":"theorem","line":1460,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L1460-L1583","doc":"**[MI-2, PROVED — the general convolution order law, sorry-free] Convolution\norder = envelope, for everywhere-nonzero factors with non-cancellation.**\nOver any normed field `K`, under the Cauchy product the q-Gevrey order is the\ndominant-balance ENVELOPE `cauchyEnvelope s t` (`= max s t` when not both\n`≤ 0`; `= s·t/(s+t)` when both `≤ 0`), NOT the additive `s + t` of the MI-1\nstub.\n\n**Both hypotheses are load-bearing, and `NoCancellation` alone is NOT\nsufficient.** The theorem needs:\n\n* `hnc : NoCancellation a b` — else the `q^{n²}` / formal-inverse pair (equal\n  positive orders, unit-sequence product of order `0`) refutes the `≥` half\n  (`not_noCancellation_of_cauchyMul_eventually_zero`);\n* `ha0`/`hb0`, the factors are **everywhere nonzero** — else the degenerate\n  `b ≡ 0` witness satisfies `NoCancellation` *vacuously* (`a ⋆ b = 0`, so\n  `c·‖a_k‖·‖b_{n-k}‖ = 0 ≤ 0`) yet breaks the conclusion (order `0 ≠ max s 0`),\n  as `cauchyMul_order_noCancellation_insufficient` proves. `NoCancellation`\n  controls the product's norm against its summands; it says nothing when a\n  *factor* vanishes.\n\n**Proof — reduction to the positive case, no separate saddle argument.** With\nthe factors nonzero the norm sequences `A = ‖a‖`, `B = ‖b‖` are strictly\npositive, so `cauchyMul_order_of_pos` gives `A ⋆ B` order `cauchyEnvelope s t`\n(that lemma already carries the interior-saddle `θ* = t/(s+t)` construction).\nThe genuine product's norm is sandwiched\n`(c/(n+1))·(A ⋆ B)_n ≤ ‖(a⋆b)_n‖ ≤ (A ⋆ B)_n` — upper by the triangle bound\n`cauchyMul_norm_le_sum`, lower by summing `hnc` over the `n+1` indices — and\nthe `n+1` factor contributes `O(log n)` to the exponent, washed out by the\n`n²` denominator. Sorry-free; axioms `[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.cauchyMul_order_of_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.cauchyMul_order_of_pos","kind":"theorem","line":1345,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L1345-L1456","doc":"**[MI-2 — PROVED for positive real coefficients] Convolution order =\nenvelope.**  The conclusion of `cauchyMul_order` on exactly the instances\n`noCancellation_of_pos` supplies, so this is a partial discharge of that\ntheorem's residual `sorry`, not a restatement of it.\n\n`≤` is `cauchyMul_bound` through the two `QGevreyBound` bridges; `≥` is the\nLaplace/saddle term selection described in the section header.  The two are glued\nby `tendsto_order`.\n\nChecked at the small instances the MI-2 numerics report: `(-1,-1) ↦ -0.5`,\n`(-2,-1) ↦ -2/3`, `(2,1) ↦ 2`, `(-3,1) ↦ 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.isQGevreyOrder_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.isQGevreyOrder_pos","kind":"theorem","line":1273,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L1273-L1281","doc":"Over `ℝ` a **positive** sequence's order can be stated without the norm. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.noCancellation_of_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.noCancellation_of_pos","kind":"theorem","line":1259,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L1259-L1271","doc":"**`NoCancellation` is satisfiable** — it holds with `c = 1` for positive real\ncoefficients.  Together with `not_noCancellation_of_cauchyMul_eventually_zero`\n(which shows it can fail) this pins the hypothesis as a genuine side condition:\nneither vacuous nor automatic. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.term_le_cauchyMul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.term_le_cauchyMul","kind":"theorem","line":1252,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L1252-L1257","doc":"**The sum dominates each of its terms** — the one place positivity is used,\nand the whole content of `noCancellation_of_pos`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.cauchyMul_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.cauchyMul_pos","kind":"theorem","line":1246,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L1246-L1250","doc":"The Cauchy product of positive sequences is positive. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.coeff_le_of_qGevreyBound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.coeff_le_of_qGevreyBound","kind":"theorem","line":1190,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L1190-L1242","doc":"**Bound form ⇒ an eventual bound on the leading coefficient.**  The `C·Aⁿ`\nprefactor contributes degree `≤ 1` in `n`, which the `n²` denominator washes\nout. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.qGevreyBound_of_isQGevreyOrder","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.qGevreyBound_of_isQGevreyOrder","kind":"theorem","line":1155,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L1155-L1188","doc":"**Limit form ⇒ bound form at an inflated slope.**  The finitely many indices\nwhere the eventual bound has not yet kicked in are absorbed into `C`; no\ngeometric factor is needed, so `A = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.slopeKCoeff_order_half","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.slopeKCoeff_order_half","kind":"theorem","line":1111,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L1111-L1116","doc":"**The slope-2 case**, `k = 2`: order `½`, level `2`. The first `k` where the\ntwo readings separate, and the level is the one that tracks the slope. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.slopeKCoeff_order_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.slopeKCoeff_order_one","kind":"theorem","line":1104,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L1104-L1109","doc":"**The slope-1 (q-Euler / Tshakaloff) case**, `k = 1`: order `1`, level `1`.\nThe two readings agree here — this is the degenerate touchstone. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.slopeKCoeff_order","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.slopeKCoeff_order","kind":"theorem","line":1070,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L1070-L1102","doc":"**The slope-`k` model has q-Gevrey order `1/k`, hence LEVEL `k` = its slope.**\n\n`L_k = z^kσ − 1` has Newton points `(0,0)`, `(1,k)`, so slope `k`. Its formal\nsolution satisfies `c_m = q^{m−k}c_{m−k}`, so `c_{jk} = q^{k j(j−1)/2}` and along\nthe nonzero subsequence `m = jk`\n\n    2·log_q c_m / m²  =  (j − 1)/(k j)  →  1/k.\n\nOrder `1/k`, level `k`. The slope is `k`. **The Newton slope is the LEVEL**, for\nevery `k ≥ 1` — not just the two instances checked before. At `k = 1` order and\nlevel coincide, which is why RSZ's own touchstone cannot see the distinction. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.logb_slopeKCoeff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.logb_slopeKCoeff","kind":"theorem","line":1055,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L1055-L1068","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.slopeKCoeff_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.slopeKCoeff_pos","kind":"theorem","line":1048,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L1048-L1053","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.slopeKCoeff_sq_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.slopeKCoeff_sq_mul","kind":"theorem","line":1033,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L1033-L1046","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.slopeKCoeff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.slopeKCoeff","kind":"def","line":1027,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L1027-L1031","doc":"Solution coefficients of the slope-`k` model `z^k σ f = f`, on the indices\n`m = jk` where they are nonzero: `d_j := c_{jk}`, from `c_m = q^{m-k}c_{m-k}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.c1InfCoeff_order_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.c1InfCoeff_order_two","kind":"theorem","line":933,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L933-L988","doc":"**The C1 `∞`-side coefficient sequence has q-Gevrey order 2.**\n\nNot the explicit function `q^{n²}` — the actual sequence defined by the\nrecursion. Squeeze on `2 log_q cₙ / n²` between the two bounds\n`(q−1)ⁿ q^{n²} ≤ cₙ qⁿ ≤ (2q−1)ⁿ q^{n²}`; both sides are\n`2 log_q(const)/n + 2 − 2/n → 2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.c1InfCoeff_upper","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.c1InfCoeff_upper","kind":"theorem","line":876,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L876-L899","doc":"`cₙ qⁿ ≤ (2q−1)ⁿ q^{n²}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.c1InfCoeff_lower","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.c1InfCoeff_lower","kind":"theorem","line":851,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L851-L874","doc":"`(q−1)ⁿ q^{n²} ≤ cₙ qⁿ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.c1InfCoeff_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.c1InfCoeff_pos","kind":"theorem","line":835,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L835-L843","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.c1InfMult_upper","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.c1InfMult_upper","kind":"theorem","line":828,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L828-L833","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.c1InfMult_lower","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.c1InfMult_lower","kind":"theorem","line":823,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L823-L826","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.c1InfCoeff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.c1InfCoeff","kind":"def","line":812,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L812-L815","doc":"The C1 `∞`-side coefficients themselves. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.c1InfMult","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.c1InfMult","kind":"def","line":808,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L808-L810","doc":"The C1 `∞`-side per-step multiplier. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.theta_eq_half_gs_bridge","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.theta_eq_half_gs_bridge","kind":"theorem","line":754,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L754-L779","doc":"**[MI-3(b), sorry-free] The θ = ½·C(m,2) bridge — leading-coefficient form.**\nThe single value `s = ½` is simultaneously (i) the q-Gevrey ORDER of the\nRoute-B kernel (its proved `q^{m²/4}` growth, `routeB_gaussian_order_half`)\nand (ii) the leading Gaussian coefficient of the GS cumulative degree\n`C(m,2)` (`gs_degree_leading_coeff_half`).  Hence the θ-coefficient of the\nkernel exponent `q^{m²/4}` is `½·s = ½·½ = ¼`, i.e. `θ = ½·C(m,2)` at leading\norder — the compiled half of the numerical probe.\n\n**CORRECTED 2026-08-08 (bean `qou-slpd` item 5).** This theorem's `s = ½` is\nthe Route-B kernel's q-Gevrey **order**, and the docstring used to call the\nmissing piece \"the RSZ slope=order theorem\". There is no such theorem: the\nNewton slope is the **level** (see §MI-3(d) below — the slope-`k` family\nseparates order from level, and the level tracks the slope). So this bridge\nreaches the right *number* `½` by the wrong route. The correct grounding of\n`ThetaSlopeArithmetic.c1Slope = ½` is the C1 `∞`-side **level**, which is\n`c1InfCoeff_order_two` restated via `isQGevreyLevel_of_order`. Nothing\ndownstream changes — Door-i needs only that the two slope multisets differ\n(`ThetaSlopeArithmetic.newtonSlopes_c1_ne_ggm`) — but the statement below\nshould be read as a numerical coincidence of two `½`s, not as a slope\nidentification. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.gs_degree_leading_coeff_half","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.gs_degree_leading_coeff_half","kind":"theorem","line":733,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L733-L752","doc":"**[MI-3(b), sorry-free] GS cumulative degree has leading coefficient ½.**\nThe Gröbner–Shirshov cumulative q-degree `C(m,2) = m(m−1)/2` (corpus\n`QOU.Mass.ThetaSlopeArithmetic.gs_cumulative_degree`) has leading Gaussian\ncoefficient `lim_{m→∞} C(m,2)/m² = ½`.  (`C(m,2)/m² = ½ − 1/(2m) → ½`.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.routeB_and_c1Inf_are_reciprocal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.routeB_and_c1Inf_are_reciprocal","kind":"theorem","line":713,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L713-L731","doc":"**The two `½`s in this corpus are `½` in reciprocal senses.**\n\n* Route-B kernel `q^{m²/4}`: order `½`, level `2`.\n* C1 `∞`-side `q^{n²}`: order `2`, level `½`.\n\nNeither file was writing a wrong number; they were writing *different\nquantities* under one word. `½ ≠ 2`, so with `order_unique` / `level_unique` no\nsingle sequence carries both, and \"the C1 slope is ½\" is ambiguous until one\nsays *order* or *level*.\n\n**Which one is the Newton-polygon slope is NOT settled here.** RSZ attaches the\n**level** to the slope (§2.3.2, Ex. 3.3.3), which would favour the C1 `∞`-side\nreading; but `theta_eq_half_gs_bridge` below grounds\n`ThetaSlopeArithmetic.c1Slope = ½` through `routeB_gaussian_order_half`, an\n**order**. Those cannot both be the slope. Deciding it needs the Newton polygon\nof `L1` computed directly — bean `qou-slpd` item 4 — not a relabelling. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.routeB_gaussian_level_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.routeB_gaussian_level_two","kind":"theorem","line":706,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L706-L711","doc":"**`q^{m²/4}` has q-Gevrey LEVEL 2.** The Route-B kernel's `½` is an\n*order*; its level is `2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.qSquare_gaussian_level_half","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.qSquare_gaussian_level_half","kind":"theorem","line":696,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L696-L704","doc":"**`q^{n²}` has q-Gevrey LEVEL ½.** Same fact as\n`qSquare_gaussian_order_two`, said in RSZ's other word. So\n`QOU.Mass.C1FormalSolutionGrowth`'s \"q-Gevrey-½ divergence\" **is correct RSZ\nterminology** — it is the *level*. It is simply not the quantity\n`IsQGevreyOrder` measures. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.qSquare_gaussian_order_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.qSquare_gaussian_order_two","kind":"theorem","line":673,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L673-L694","doc":"**`q^{n²}` has q-Gevrey order 2** (and hence level `½`).\n\nStated because two files in this corpus attach the number `½` to `q^{m²/4}` and\nto `q^{n²}`, which are reciprocal:\n\n* `routeB_gaussian_order_half` (above) — growth `q^{m²/4}`, **order** `½`.\n* `QOU.Mass.C1FormalSolutionGrowth` — growth `q^{n²}` for the C1 `∞`-side\n  formal solution, docstring \"the q-Gevrey-½ divergence\" — that `½` is the\n  **level** (RSZ §1.3.2), i.e. order `2`.\n\n`IsQGevreyOrder q s a` is `‖aₙ‖ ∼ Cⁿ q^{s n²/2}`, which is RSZ's *order*\nverbatim, so `q^{n²}` forces `s = 2`. Neither file wrote a wrong number; they\nwrote **different quantities** under one word. See\n`routeB_and_c1Inf_are_reciprocal` for what that leaves open, and the\nlevel section above for the RSZ citations. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.level_unique","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.level_unique","kind":"theorem","line":666,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L666-L671","doc":"**Level is a well-defined invariant** for nonzero levels, from `order_unique`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.isQGevreyLevel_of_order","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.isQGevreyLevel_of_order","kind":"theorem","line":660,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L660-L664","doc":"A sequence of order `s ≠ 0` has level `1/s`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.isQGevreyLevel_iff_order","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.isQGevreyLevel_iff_order","kind":"theorem","line":656,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L656-L658","doc":"Level and order are reciprocal, by definition. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.IsQGevreyLevel","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.IsQGevreyLevel","kind":"def","line":653,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L653-L654","doc":"**q-Gevrey level** (RSZ §1.3.2): level `d` means order `1/d`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.routeB_gaussian_order_half","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.routeB_gaussian_order_half","kind":"theorem","line":622,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L622-L632","doc":"**[MI-3(b), sorry-free] Route-B kernel has q-Gevrey order ½.**  The proved\nRoute-B growth `q^{m²/4}` is q-Gevrey order `½`, since\n`q^{m²/4} = q^{(½)·m²/2}` matches the order-`½` Gaussian.  This grounds\n`ThetaSlopeArithmetic.c1Slope = ½` for the actual coefficient sequence. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.order_of_rpow_gaussian","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.order_of_rpow_gaussian","kind":"theorem","line":599,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L599-L620","doc":"**[MI-3(b), sorry-free] Explicit Gaussian sequence has its named order.**\nThe real sequence `n ↦ q^{s n²/2}` (`Real.rpow`) has q-Gevrey order exactly\n`s`, for any `q > 1`.  This is the concrete realiser that turns the abstract\nslope `s` into an actual coefficient sequence: `logb q (q^{s n²/2}) = s n²/2`,\nso the leading coefficient `2·log_q‖a n‖/n² = s` for every `n ≥ 1`, a\nconstant sequence whose limit is `s`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.order_eventually_ge","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.order_eventually_ge","kind":"theorem","line":588,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L588-L597","doc":"**[MI-3(a), sorry-free] Limit form ⟹ eventual lower bound.**  Dually, for\nevery `ε > 0` the leading Gaussian coefficient is eventually `≥ s − ε`.\nTogether with `order_eventually_le` this is the full two-sided bound form. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.order_eventually_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.order_eventually_le","kind":"theorem","line":575,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L575-L586","doc":"**[MI-3(a), sorry-free] Limit form ⟹ eventual upper bound.**  If `a` has\nq-Gevrey order `s`, then for every `ε > 0` the leading Gaussian coefficient\nis eventually `≤ s + ε`.  Equivalently `‖a n‖ ≤ q^{(s+ε) n²/2}` eventually —\nthe one-sided envelope the `≤` direction of `cauchyMul_order` extracts from\nthe defining limit. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.cauchyMul_leadingCoeff_ge_endpoint","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.cauchyMul_leadingCoeff_ge_endpoint","kind":"theorem","line":467,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L467-L545","doc":"**[MI-2, sorry-free] Endpoint `≥`-brick for the convolution order law.**\nThe lower (`≥`) half of `cauchyMul_order`, discharged unconditionally at the\n**`k = n` endpoint** — the pick that attains the envelope `max s t` when `a`\ndominates (`t ≤ s`, so `cauchyEnvelope s t = s`).\n\nGiven the non-cancellation hypothesis `hnc`, a nonzero constant term\n`b 0 ≠ 0`, and `a` eventually nonzero, the leading Gaussian coefficient of\n`a ⋆ b` is eventually `≥ s − ε`.  The single summand `a n · b 0` already\ngrows like `q^{s n²/2}`, and `hnc` transfers that growth to the whole\nconvolution: `‖(a⋆b)_n‖ ≥ c·‖a_n‖·‖b_0‖`, whence\n\n`2·log_q‖(a⋆b)_n‖/n² ≥ 2·log_q‖a_n‖/n² + 2(log_q c + log_q‖b_0‖)/n² → s`,\n\nthe second term being `O(1/n²)`.  This closes the `θ* = 1` pick the general\n`cauchyMul_order` `sorry` cites, for the physically relevant `s`-dominant\npositive-order regime (e.g. a Route-B kernel of order `½` against a\nlower-order factor).  It does **not** close the general theorem: the\ninterior-optimum case (both orders `≤ 0`, envelope `s t/(s+t)`) still needs a\nnear-optimal `k ≈ θ*·n` argument.  Companion of `order_eventually_ge`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.not_noCancellation_of_cauchyMul_eventually_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.not_noCancellation_of_cauchyMul_eventually_zero","kind":"theorem","line":417,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L417-L440","doc":"**The non-cancellation hypothesis has teeth.**  If the convolution\neventually vanishes while some summand does not, `NoCancellation` fails —\nso it is not satisfied automatically, and the `≥` direction genuinely\ndepends on it.\n\nThis is the shape of the counterexample recorded in\n`computations/qbeta/cauchymul_order_cancellation_witness.py`: taking\n`a n = q^{n²}` (positive order) and `b` its formal inverse (the computation\nshows `log_q‖b n‖/n² → 1`, so `b` carries the SAME positive order), the\nCauchy product is the unit sequence `(1,0,0,…)` — order `0`, while\n`cauchyEnvelope s t > 0` for `s, t > 0`.  Two factors of equal positive\norder whose product has order `0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.NoCancellation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.NoCancellation","kind":"def","line":403,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L403-L415","doc":"**Non-cancellation of the dominant convolution term.**  There is a fixed\n`c > 0` such that, eventually, the convolution dominates *every* one of its\nown summands: `c·‖a_k‖·‖b_{n−k}‖ ≤ ‖(a⋆b)_n‖` for all `k ≤ n`.\n\nThis is the hypothesis the `≥` direction of the order law needs, and it is a\ngenuine hypothesis, not bookkeeping: it constrains only the *norm* of the\nproduct against the norms of its summands, saying nothing about the product's\norder, so assuming it does not assume the conclusion.  In a general normed\nfield the summands can cancel; `not_noCancellation_of_cauchyMul_eventually_zero`\nbelow is the extreme case, and `q^{n²} ⋆ (q^{n²})⁻¹` realises it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.cauchyMul_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.cauchyMul_bound","kind":"theorem","line":319,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L319-L401","doc":"**[MI-2, sorry-free] The `≤` direction of the convolution law.**  In bound\nform the upper half of `cauchyMul_order` holds **unconditionally** — no\nnon-cancellation hypothesis, because bounding a sum of norms never needs to\nknow that the terms fail to cancel.\n\nThis is the half that survives the counterexample: only the `≥` direction\nwas false. The proof is the docstring's own recipe with nothing deferred —\n`cauchyMul_norm_le_sum` for the triangle bound, `exponent_le_envelope` on\neach summand's Gaussian exponent, and the `(n+1)` term count absorbed into\nthe geometric factor via `n + 1 ≤ 2ⁿ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.QGevreyBound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.QGevreyBound","kind":"def","line":312,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L312-L317","doc":"**Bound form of q-Gevrey growth.**  `‖a n‖ ≤ C·Aⁿ·q^{s n²/2}` — the\n\"ALTERNATIVE (bound form)\" of the header, made a definition.  Unlike\n`IsQGevreyOrder` this is finitary (no filter, no `log`), which is exactly why\nthe `≤` half of the convolution law is provable outright in this form. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.envelope_shift_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.envelope_shift_le","kind":"theorem","line":251,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L251-L310","doc":"**[MI-2, sorry-free] Envelope shift bound.**  Raising both slopes by the same\n`δ ≥ 0` raises the envelope by at most `δ`.\n\nThis is the algebraic core of the `ε`-`δ` step: passing from the limit form\nto a bound costs an `ε` in each slope, and this lemma says that costs at most\n`ε` in the envelope — so the two forms have the same envelope in the limit.\nConceptually it is `sup_θ((s+δ)θ² + (t+δ)(1−θ)²) ≤ sup_θ(sθ² + t(1−θ)²) + δ`,\nsince `θ² + (1−θ)² ≤ 1` on `[0,1]`; proved here directly from the closed\nform, which is why it is a four-way case split rather than one line. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.cauchyMul_norm_le_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.cauchyMul_norm_le_sum","kind":"theorem","line":239,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L239-L249","doc":"**[MI-2 ingredient] Triangle bound for the Cauchy product (proved\nsorry-free).**  `‖(a⋆b)_n‖ ≤ ∑_{k≤n} ‖a_k‖·‖b_{n-k}‖`.  Combined with\n`exponent_le_envelope` this supplies the `≤` (upper) direction of the order\nlaw: each summand's exponent is envelope-bounded, and the `(n+1)`-fold sum\nis washed out by the `n²` denominator. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.exponent_le_envelope","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.exponent_le_envelope","kind":"theorem","line":203,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L203-L237","doc":"**[MI-2 core] Dominant-balance inequality (proved sorry-free).**  For\n`k, j ≥ 0` the raw Gaussian exponent is bounded by the envelope:\n`s·k² + t·j² ≤ cauchyEnvelope s t · (k + j)²`.  This is the analytic heart\nof the `≤` direction of the convolution order law — a completing-the-square\nidentity in each regime:\n\n* both `s,t ≤ 0`, `s+t < 0`:\n  `(s k²+t j²)(s+t) − s t (k+j)² = (s k − t j)² ≥ 0`, and dividing by\n  `s+t < 0` flips to `s k²+t j² ≤ s t/(s+t)·(k+j)²`;\n* otherwise: `max s t ≥ 0`, and\n  `max s t·(k+j)² − (s k²+t j²) = (max−s)k² + (max−t)j² + max·2kj ≥ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.cauchyEnvelope","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.cauchyEnvelope","kind":"def","line":194,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L194-L201","doc":"**The Cauchy-product q-Gevrey envelope** `E(s,t) = sup_{θ∈[0,1]}(sθ²+t(1−θ)²)`\nin closed form: the harmonic combination `s·t/(s+t)` when both slopes are\n`≤ 0` (concave, interior saddle — the convergent-q-Gevrey regime), and\n`max s t` otherwise (convex, endpoint-dominated).  At `s = t = 0` both\nbranches give `0` (`0/0 = 0` in Lean).  Replaces the incorrect additive\n`s + t` of the MI-1 stub. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.cauchyMul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.cauchyMul","kind":"def","line":190,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L190-L192","doc":"The Cauchy (convolution) product of two coefficient sequences. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.prod_adds","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.prod_adds","kind":"theorem","line":122,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L122-L143","doc":"**Pointwise product adds order.**  If `a` has order `s`, `b` has order `t`,\nand both are eventually nonzero, then the pointwise product `n ↦ a n · b n`\nhas order `s + t`.  This grounds `ThetaSlopeArithmetic.prodSlope s t = s + t`\nfor actual sequences: the theta exponents multiply,\n`q^{s n²/2}·q^{t n²/2} = q^{(s+t)n²/2}`, hence the leading coefficients add.\n\nHypotheses `ha0`/`hb0` (eventual nonvanishing) are needed for the `log`\nadditivity `log(xy)=log x+log y`; they are automatic for the nonzero\ncoefficient sequences that carry a genuine slope. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.geometric_order_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.geometric_order_zero","kind":"theorem","line":100,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L100-L120","doc":"**Geometric sequences have order 0.**  For any `C : K`, the geometric\nsequence `n ↦ C^n` has q-Gevrey order `0` (it carries only the `C^n`\nprefactor, no Gaussian `n²` term).  This grounds\n`ThetaSlopeArithmetic.c1Slope`-style order bookkeeping: pure exponential\ngrowth is order-0, the neutral element of the slope algebra. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.bar_negates","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.bar_negates","kind":"theorem","line":81,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L81-L98","doc":"**Bar involution negates order.**  The same sequence `a`, measured against\nthe bar-conjugate base `q⁻¹`, has order `-s`.  This grounds\n`ThetaSlopeArithmetic.bar (s) = -s` at the level of actual sequences: the\nbar map `q ↦ q⁻¹` inverts the q-Gevrey growth direction. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.logb_inv_base","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.logb_inv_base","kind":"theorem","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L74-L79","doc":"Pointwise: `log_{q⁻¹} x = - log_q x` (holds unconditionally, since\n`log q⁻¹ = -log q` and `x/(-y) = -(x/y)` even when `log q = 0`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.order_unique","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.order_unique","kind":"theorem","line":67,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L67-L72","doc":"**Order is a well-defined invariant.**  When the q-Gevrey order exists it is\nunique — two witnessed orders of the same sequence agree.  (Uniqueness of a\nlimit in a Hausdorff space.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.IsQGevreyOrder","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyOrder.IsQGevreyOrder","kind":"def","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyOrder.lean#L59-L65","doc":"The q-Gevrey order of a coefficient sequence `a : ℕ → K`, measured against a\nbase `q` (`q > 1`).  `a` has order `s` when `‖a n‖ ∼ C^n · q^{s·n²/2}`, made\nprecise as the limit of the leading Gaussian coefficient\n`(2·log_q ‖a n‖)/n² → s`.  The `atTop` filter and the `n²` denominator wash\nout the `C^n` prefactor and the finitely many `n = 0,1` terms. ","signatureChunk":0}]}